meric":9.14}}],"logp":[{"abbreviation_in_the_article":"Ir3","value":1.92,"value_relation":"=","std":0.2,"type_authors":"logP","method":"shake-flask","aqueous_phase":"water","doi":"10.1021/acs.jmedchem.4c00280","year":2024,"counterion":"[Cl-]","record_id":"MLDB-003733","detection":"UV-Vis","method_description":"The log p values of complexes Ir1–Ir3 were determined using the shake-flask method, which employs the lipophilicity of n-octanol to mimic the lipid bilayer of a cell membrane. In this method, the sample was dissolved in a mixture of equal volumes of n-octanol and water and shaken overnight to facilitate partitioning. Subsequently, the sample was centrifuged at 5000 rpm for 10 min, and the aqueous layer was meticulously separated from the octanol layer for analysis. The concentrations of complexes in the aqueous phase and octanol layer were quantified by using UV–Vis spectroscopy. The partition coefficients of iridium complexes were then calculated by using the following equation: logP = log([octanol] / [water])"}]}